# Video

A `<video>` gets controls unless the author asks for something else, and its
fallback content is a link — @video(tiny.mp4)[a tiny clip]{width=0.2} even
inline.

@video+(tiny.mp4)[A run of the model]{width=0.6 poster=frame.png}

@video+(tiny.mp4)[Muted and looping]{width=0.4 poster=frame.png loop muted controls=false}

In LaTeX a video degrades under `Video mode`, per-video via `{tex-mode=…}`.

@video+(tiny.mp4)[Embedded]{width=8cm height=6cm poster=frame.png tex-mode=embed}

@video+(tiny.mp4)[Attached]{width=0.4 poster=frame.png tex-mode=attach}

@video+(tiny.mp4)[Poster only]{width=0.4 poster=frame.png tex-mode=poster}

@video+(tiny.mp4)[Linked]{width=0.4 poster=frame.png tex-mode=link}

A remote clip cannot be embedded in a PDF, so it falls back to a link.

@video+(https://example.com/clip.mp4)[Remote]{width=0.4 tex-mode=embed}
